  :adr/title "git.kotobase.net: reclaim custom domain from stray concurrent-session Worker"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/decided "2026-07-18"
  :adr/body "## Context

ADR-2607177500 (2026-07-17) recorded an incident: `kotobase-protocols-worker`
had `git.kotobase.net` as a Cloudflare custom-domain route, but a concurrent
session's own Worker (Cloudflare service name `kotobase-git`) declared the
same custom domain in its own `wrangler deploy` mid-session. Cloudflare
custom domains are single-owner — the last `wrangler deploy` to declare a
route silently wins, with no conflict surfaced to either side. That ADR
chose NOT to force-reclaim it, citing this workspace's 並行エージェント運用
guardrail against destructive interference with a concurrent session's
in-progress work, and instead routed the git surface only through
`kotobase-protocols-worker.*.workers.dev/git/*`, leaving the `git.` hostname
contested and unresolved.

Root owner asked (2026-07-18) whether `net-kotobase`/`kotoba-lang/kotobase`
and `git.kotobase.net` were actually designed and working. Investigation
found `kotobase.net` itself live and correct (HTTP 200, ADR-2607184000
production evidence), but confirmed `git.kotobase.net` was still owned by
the impostor `kotobase-git` Worker and served \"repository not found\" for
the previously-seeded test repo — the incident from ADR-2607177500 had not
been resolved in the ~21 hours since.

## Decision

Reclaim the `git.kotobase.net` custom domain for `kotobase-protocols-worker`,
after first confirming it was safe to do so:

- `wrangler deployments list --name kotobase-git` shows the same Cloudflare
  account/OAuth identity (`04.feasts_minded@icloud.com`) as this workspace's
  own sessions — not an external third party.
- Zero deploys on `kotobase-git` since 2026-07-17T12:01:58Z (a ~2h43m burst
  of 10 deploys that day, then nothing) — consistent with an abandoned or
  completed experiment, not active in-progress work.
- `kotobase-git` is registered nowhere in this superproject's
  `manifest/repos.edn` or `manifest/west.yml` — not a tracked project, and
  grepping `90-docs/adr` + `orgs/` for \"kotobase-git\" turns up only this
  repo's own incident documentation.
- Root owner explicitly directed the reclaim after being shown this
  evidence.

Re-added `{ \"pattern\": \"git.kotobase.net\", \"custom_domain\": true }` to
`kotobase-protocols-worker`'s `wrangler.jsonc` routes and redeployed — the
same last-deploy-wins mechanism that took the domain in the first place.

## Verification

- `npm run build` (shadow-cljs release worker) and full test suite green
  before deploy: nbb pure-logic tests (12 tests/43 assertions) + node-test
  real-crypto suite (20 tests/68 assertions, CACAO verify + datom-store
  bridge), consistent with this workspace's rule to verify build/tests
  before any production deploy.
- Post-deploy, `wrangler deploy` output listed
  `git.kotobase.net (custom domain)` as a trigger of
  `kotobase-protocols-worker`.
- `curl https://git.kotobase.net/kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack`
  now returns the same git-protocol-aware `repository not found:
  kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols` response as the known-good
  `https://kotobase-protocols-worker.<account>.workers.dev/git/...` path —
  proving the domain now points at the correct Worker. (The \"repository not
  found\" itself is expected and unrelated: the storage backend migrated to
  a real content-addressed datom-plane per ADR-2607177500, and the earlier
  test seed was not carried over — reseeding via `bin/seed_git.cljs` is a
  separate, optional follow-up, not required to prove the routing fix.)

## Addendum (same day, follow-up reseed attempt): separate data-integrity bug found

Attempted the optional reseed as a stronger end-to-end proof (`bin/seed_git.cljs`
against this repo's own 74 objects/5 refs). This surfaced an unrelated,
pre-existing bug in `kotobase-protocols-worker`'s shared content-addressed
store (affects all surfaces — s3/atproto/git/pinning — not just git), found
independently of the domain-routing fix above:

- The bulk seed run completed all 74 object PUTs and 5 ref PUTs without any
  reported failure (only the final local `git symbolic-ref HEAD` step failed,
  because the shared west checkout was in detached-HEAD state — fixed by
  PUTting the HEAD symref content directly).
- `git clone` then failed: `Unable to find 936ce326… ` (404) for one specific
  commit object, even though the bulk seed reported no error for it.
- Manually re-PUTting that one object returned 200, and repeated `curl GET`
  (including 5 concurrent requests) consistently returned the correct
  900-byte content, which decodes to exactly the expected SHA
  (`936ce326009f55f17dfb57f3dea30fb0ca280544`, verified by reconstructing the
  proper `type len` + NUL byte + `content` header and hashing).
- `git clone` still failed on retry: `has bad hash`. `GIT_TRACE_CURL=1`
  showed why — the *exact same URL*, requested moments later in the same
  clone attempt, returned `content-length: 91` instead of 900 (a different,
  wrong body), while a `curl` immediately after that clone attempt again got
  900 correct bytes. The same GET endpoint is **non-deterministically
  returning different content for the same object key** across successive
  requests seconds apart.
- This is consistent with a race in the write path's CAS/fold cycle
  (`kotobase-protocols-worker.kotobase-store`, ADR-2607177500) under
  concurrent writes to the shared admin graph (`kotobase-protocols-v2`) —
  e.g. a losing writer's change appears to succeed (200) but a later read
  transiently rehydrates from a stale/incomplete chain state. This was not
  investigated further or fixed here: it touches the shared datom-plane
  persistence bridge used by every surface of this Worker, is live
  production infrastructure other consumers may depend on, and a blind fix
  attempted without dedicated time to test concurrent-write scenarios risks
  making a live data-integrity bug worse rather than better.
- **Recommendation for follow-up**: treat as a P1 data-integrity bug in
  `kotobase-protocols-worker.kotobase-store`'s hydrate/commit/fold cycle,
  not a git-surface-specific issue. Needs reproduction under controlled
  concurrent writes before attempting a fix.

### Addendum 2 (same session): root-cause investigation, inconclusive

Read the full pipeline end to end — `kotobase.protocols.git`
(`kotobase-protocols/src/kotobase/protocols/git.cljc`: `loose-object`/
`handle-put`), `kotobase-protocols-worker.kotobase-store`
(`hydrate!`/`diff->tx-data!`/`commit-changes!`/`fold-if-due!`/
`hydrate-run-persist!`), `kotobase-protocols-worker.kotobase-r2` (the
isolate-lifetime `block-cache`, `with-blocks` trampoline,
`r2-get-head`/`r2-put-head-if-match` CAS), and `kotobase-peer.core`
(`commit!`, `fold!`, `chain.core/commit!`). Findings:

- The write-side OCC design is structurally sound as written: the R2 head
  pointer is read fresh every request (never cached), blocks are
  content-addressed and flushed to R2 *before* the head CAS, a CAS loss
  (`persisted false`) triggers a full re-hydrate-and-retry (≤3 attempts,
  `worker.cljs`'s `with-retries`), and `crypto/encrypt-fn` here is a
  deterministic plaintext passthrough — the exact precondition `fold!`'s
  own docstring requires for concurrent folds to converge and never lose
  data.
  No single-line logic bug was found in this pass.
- Confirmed via `_diag/health` that no concurrent writer is active on the
  shared graph right now (`novelty_size` held at 3 across 3 checks, several
  seconds apart) — ruling out an *ongoing* external writer as the cause of
  what was observed.
- Attempted reproduction twice, both failed to reproduce:
  1. 20 concurrent `GET`s for the same already-corrupted-then-fixed object
     → 20/20 consistent, correct 900 bytes.
  2. One `PUT` immediately followed by 20 concurrent `GET`s of that same
     new key → 20/20 consistent, correct content.
  Both used `wrangler tail` in parallel; zero exceptions, zero suspicious
  log lines were captured in either run.
- The object that actually broke was written partway through a **74-object
  sequential seed burst**, well past the engine's 64-tx-block fold
  threshold (`kotobase-peer.core/should-fold?`) — meaning exactly one
  `fold!` (`kotobase-peer/src/kotobase_peer/core.cljc:2050`, the single
  most complex, least-frequently-exercised path in this pipeline: a full
  snapshot rehydrate + novelty reduce + resnapshot, vs. every other path's
  simple point read/write) almost certainly ran mid-burst. Neither repro
  attempt above involved enough writes to cross that threshold, which is
  the most likely reason both came back clean — a plain point-write/read
  race was not reproducible, but the fold path itself was never actually
  exercised by either repro.
- **Net assessment**: no confirmed root cause. The strongest remaining
  hypothesis, unconfirmed, is a `fold!`-cycle correctness bug specific to
  folding a large novelty backlog written by a rapid, purely-sequential
  (not concurrently-racing) single client — i.e. the bug may not require
  concurrent *writers* at all, only enough sequential volume to cross the
  fold threshold once. Confirming this needs a repro of ≥65 sequential
  writes to a scratch graph/repo with `wrangler tail` attached and,
  ideally, an assertion in `fold!` that every pre-fold novelty quad is
  present in the post-fold indexed snapshot (a property `verify-chain`,
  `kotobase-peer/src/kotobase_peer/core.cljc:2179`, does not currently
  check). Not attempted here — a ≥65-write repro takes the store's current
  ~1s+/write cost past 10 minutes per attempt (matches this session's own
  74-object seed, which took roughly that long), and iterating on a fix
  hypothesis at that cost per cycle was judged better handed to a
  dedicated follow-up than continued in this session.

## Consequences

- (+) `git.kotobase.net` now serves the intended
  `kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols` git dumb-HTTP surface, closing the gap
  ADR-2607177500 left open.
- (+) `wrangler.jsonc` and `README.md` in `kotobase-protocols-worker` updated
  to drop the now-stale \"contested, workers.dev only\" language.
- (-) The `kotobase-git` Worker itself was not deleted (only the custom
  domain route moved) — if it is in fact someone's abandoned experiment, its
  Worker script still exists under that name, unrouted.
- (-) No data was reseeded onto `git.kotobase.net` as part of this fix; the
  previously-verified test clone (ADR-2607176000) is not currently
  reproducible without a fresh `bin/seed_git.cljs` run.

